course: religion-ethics
lesson: 84
title: Kind truths: honesty with a gentle voice
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A kind truth is something true said in a way that helps: in private, at the right time, with care for the person who hears it.
explain: Ollie's mate has started wearing a hat he thinks gives him "rizz" (charm, being cool), and everyone laughs behind his back; a quiet, kind word from Ollie is better than letting him be laughed at.
explain: Kind truths often mix three things: notice the real good, say the true thing gently, and offer help, like "the chorus is catchy, the drums need work, want to practise?"
explain: Christians are told to go to a friend privately first (Matthew 18:15) and to "encourage one another and build each other up" (1 Thessalonians 5:11), so honesty and love travel together.
explain: Watch out for half-lies: vague words meant to mislead, or "just joking" after a hurtful lie, are not honest, even if every word sounds clever.
fact: Leviticus 19:17 says to "rebuke your neighbour frankly" rather than hate him in your heart, and Matthew 18:15 says to go to him privately first.
fact: Aristotle's middle way: a true friend speaks up when it matters, but kindly and in private.
fact: Coaches say criticism helps in practice, not at the starting line; before a show, encourage, and after it, talk about getting better.
fact: Christians ask whether words are meant to mislead, not only whether they are technically true: "Let your 'Yes' be 'Yes'" (Matthew 5:37).
fact: Some philosophers say tricky true words are better than a lie; others say any attempt to mislead is the same wrong.
fact: "Mourn with those who mourn" (Romans 12:15): when someone is sad, comfort comes first.
fact: Proverbs 26:18-19 warns against someone who deceives a neighbour and then says, "I was only joking!"; a joke both people get is not deception.
fact: You don't have to repeat every hurt you know: "Whoever would foster love covers over an offence, but whoever repeats the matter separates close friends" (Proverbs 17:9); but don't say "no" if it's false.
fact: Playful exaggeration everyone sees through, like "Ten!" for burnt pancakes, is not really deceiving; real questions deserve real answers.
fact: Sharing what you hope and believe, as a hope, isn't lying; claiming certainty you don't have is different.
fact: Christians believe God cares for every creature, even a sparrow (Matthew 10:29).
fact: "Stand up in the presence of the aged, show respect for the elderly" (Leviticus 19:32); older people learn new technology well when helped patiently.
fact: Name the real good bits ("the pancakes were amazing") instead of a white lie, and save the rest for a quiet word later.
fact: Slang check: "it's giving" means "it gives a vibe"; "yeah nah" means no; "sweet as" means great; in Wales "mamgu" means gran and "cariad" means love.
exercise: Everyone laughs behind Ollie's mate's back about his hat. What is the kind truth? | Tell him quietly and kindly, in private | Laugh along so he doesn't feel left out | Tell the whole class to stop laughing at him | Say nothing and let him find out himself
exercise: What does "rizz" mean? | Charm, being cool | A kind of woolly hat | Being rude to people | Being very, very rich
exercise: Zoe's friend sings tomorrow and is off key. "Should I go for it?" What is best tonight? | Encourage her, maybe help with one tricky bit, and talk honestly after the show | Tell her she sings badly and should drop out right now | Say she's the best singer in the world | Tell the teacher to cancel her turn
exercise: Ethan found Grandma's boring, and Grandma is listening when Mum asks. What is wise? | Name the real good bits, and tell Mum the rest quietly later | Say loudly that it was the most boring day ever, so that everyone hears the truth | Say it was the best day of his whole life | Walk off without answering anybody
exercise: Jade says her cousin's shoes are "giving... a lot". What does "it's giving" mean? | It gives a vibe | It is a present | It is too expensive | It is broken
exercise: Is a vague answer like "it's giving... a lot" honest if you mean to mislead? | Not really; dodging to mislead is half a lie | Yes, it is always honest if each word is true | Yes, because slang words never count as lies | No, because all vague answers are terrible lies
exercise: Max's friend lost the race: "Was I really slow?" Which reply is true and kind? | You were slower today, but you gave it everything, I saw you | No, you were the fastest really, the others all cheated at the start, I saw them | Yes, you were the slowest by miles | Running doesn't matter, nobody cares about races
exercise: Tama tells his aunty, "Yeah nah, your sausage rolls are sweet as," then admits they were a bit burnt. What does "yeah nah" mean? | No | Yes | Maybe | Thanks
exercise: When is a joke honest? | When both people know it's a joke and nobody is deceived | Whenever you say "just joking" afterwards | Only when it is about yourself | Never, jokes are always a kind of lie
exercise: Poppy asks if Lily said anything mean. Lily did, it was small, and Lily already said sorry. What is honest without spreading hurt? | I don't want to pass stuff on; talk to Lily if you're worried | No, Lily never says anything about you | Yes, and here is exactly what she said, word for word, and who she said it to at lunch | Lily hates you, everyone knows that
exercise: Dad burns the pancakes, Jayden says "Ten!" and Dad laughs, "Liar." Why is it hardly a lie? | It's playful exaggeration that everyone sees through | Because the pancakes really were perfect and golden | Because lying to parents is allowed at breakfast time, as long as you are being nice | Because Dad did not hear what Jayden said
exercise: Mia's friend's dog died: "Do you think he knew I loved him?" What is honest comfort? | He wagged every time you came home; I think he knew | I'm sure he didn't, because dogs can't really feel love for people the way we do | I know for certain, a vet told me | I don't know, let's talk about something else
exercise: Bethan's mamgu asks, "Am I too old for this phone, cariad?" What is true, respectful and useful? | Nobody's too old, it's just new. I'll show you | Yes, you're far too old for phones now, so give it to me instead | Of course not, you're perfect at it already | Ask somebody else, I'm busy
exercise: What does "cariad" mean in Welsh? | Love | Phone | Granny | Hurry
exercise: Which Bible verse tells you to go to someone privately first? | Matthew 18:15 | Genesis 1:1 | Psalm 23:1, the Lord is my shepherd | John 3:16
exercise: What does "Mourn with those who mourn" (Romans 12:15) teach? | When someone is sad, comfort comes first | Never cry in front of other people, even family | Mourning is only for grown-ups, never for children | Tell sad people to cheer up quickly and move on
test: Your mate says, "Being honest means saying what you think, loud, whenever." What is missing? | Kind honesty also thinks about when, where and how it is said | Nothing; louder truth is always better truth, because then nobody can miss it | Honesty means never saying what you think | You should only be honest with teachers
test: "Oi, your hat's well cringe, mate, everyone's laughing," Ollie shouts across the yard. What did he do wrong? | He told the truth in public and harshly instead of privately and kindly | He should have lied and said the hat was cool | Nothing; shouting the truth is the kindest way | He should have told the teacher about the hat
test: A cartoon shows a coach yelling at a runner on the starting line: "Your form is terrible!" What is wrong? | Criticism helps in practice, not at the starting line | Nothing; that is the perfect time for criticism, when the runner is listening hardest | Coaches should never say anything about form | The runner should yell back at the coach
test: Your sister says, "If I say 'it's giving a lot' about her shoes, I didn't lie, so I'm fine." What is the best reply? | If you're trying to mislead her, it's half a lie; say what you honestly can, like "not my style, but they suit you" | Yes, any true words are always perfectly honest | No, you must tell her the shoes are ugly | Say nothing and walk away from her
test: Your friend jokes, "Your project's trash," you look hurt, and he says, "Just joking!" What does Proverbs 26:18-19 warn about? | Hurting someone and then hiding behind "I was only joking" | Telling jokes that are much too long and boring for anyone to laugh at | Laughing too loudly in class | Never telling any jokes at all
test: Poppy pushes: "Just tell me, did Lily say something?" You know she did. Is "No" OK? | No; you need not repeat it, but don't say something false | Yes, saying no is always kind, because it stops Poppy from feeling hurt | Yes, lies to friends don't count | No, you must tell her every word exactly
test: What would happen if everyone always said "You were the best!" to friends who lost? | Praise would stop meaning anything and nobody would know how to improve | Everyone would become faster runners by next week, because they'd feel so good about themselves | Nobody would ever feel sad again about losing | Races would be cancelled because everyone won
test: Mia's friend asks about her dog, and Mia says, "I know for sure he's in heaven, a vet told me." What is the problem? | She claims a certainty she doesn't have; sharing a hope is honest, inventing proof is not | There is no problem; it is always good to comfort people | Vets are not allowed to talk about heaven | She should never say anything about the dog
test: Mamgu laughs, "Too old for this phone, I am!" Your brother sniggers. What is the best thing to do? | Show her patiently; nobody is too old to learn | Laugh along with your brother, because she's so slow | Take the phone away from her so she can't break it | Tell her she's right and that she should give up on it
test: In Auckland, "sweet as" means what? | Great, fine | Sugary and sticky | Sad and lonely | Much too sweet to eat
test: Josh's aunt asks, "Did you enjoy my piano recital?" It was long, but her last piece was lovely. What is the kind truth? | That last piece was lovely! | It was the best concert in history! | It went on for ages, honestly | I fell asleep in the middle
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a "kind truth" as described in this lesson? | A truthful statement delivered privately, at a fitting moment, with regard for the welfare of the hearer | A falsehood uttered for the purpose of preserving the hearer's contentment | A truthful statement delivered publicly with the intention of humiliating the hearer | A deliberately vague statement intended to create a false impression
exam: Select the response consistent with the teaching of Matthew 18:15 concerning a friend's fault. | The friend ought first to be approached privately and spoken to directly | The fault ought first to be announced before the assembled class, so that all may learn from it | The friend ought to be avoided until the fault is forgotten | The fault ought to be reported anonymously to a stranger
exam: Given that a friend is to perform publicly on the following day, identify the course of action most consistent with the counsel of coaches as cited. | The provision of encouragement beforehand, candid criticism being reserved for a later occasion | The provision of severe criticism immediately prior to the performance | The provision of extravagant false praise both before and after | The withdrawal of the friend from the performance without consultation
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning an expression of hope, as distinct from an assertion of certainty, is consistent with this lesson. | The sharing of a hope, presented as a hope, does not constitute a falsehood | The sharing of any hope invariably constitutes a falsehood, since the future cannot be known | An assertion of certainty is required whenever comfort is offered | Neither hope nor certainty may be expressed to a bereaved person
